<h2>Wind Coverage: Homeowners Policy or TWIA?</h2>
<p>Because Jefferson County is a TWIA first-tier coastal county, many Port Arthur owners carry a homeowners policy with a wind and hail exclusion plus a separate TWIA windstorm policy. Others have wind coverage through their homeowners carrier. Your declarations page tells you which. The answer matters because each insurer has different claim procedures, timelines, and deductibles. If TWIA is your wind insurer, see our <a href="/services/twia-windstorm-claims/">TWIA windstorm claims</a> page.</p>
<p>Many Texas policies use a wind/hail deductible expressed as a percentage of the dwelling limit, and some have a separate named-storm deductible. A thunderstorm loss and a hurricane loss on the same home can carry different deductibles.</p>

[[cta-storm]]

<h2>How Insurers Push Back on Wind Claims</h2>
<p>Wind claims are often decided on cause. Common positions we see include:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>“Wear and tear.”</strong> The adjuster attributes missing shingles or brittle tabs to age and deterioration.</li>
<li><strong>“Wind speeds were not high enough.”</strong> The carrier relies on a distant weather station rather than local reports and visible damage.</li>
<li><strong>“Installation defect.”</strong> Shingles that came off are blamed on improper nailing rather than wind.</li>
<li><strong>“Repairable.”</strong> The estimate replaces a handful of shingles when brittle materials cannot be lifted and repaired without more damage, or when matching shingles are unavailable.</li>
<li><strong>“Interior water is a separate leak.”</strong> Rain damage inside the home is treated as long-term leakage instead of storm-created openings.</li>
</ul>
<p>If a wind claim was denied, ask the insurer to explain the denial in writing and identify the policy language it used. Our <a href="/services/denied-insurance-claims/">denied insurance claims</a> page explains next steps, and <a href="/services/insurance-estimate-review/">insurance estimate review</a> covers low estimates.</p>

<h2>Steps to Take After Wind Damage</h2>
<ol>
<li>Make sure the property is safe. Stay away from downed power lines and damaged structures.</li>
<li>Photograph and video the damage from the ground and, if safe, from closer views. Include debris in the yard.</li>
<li>Make temporary repairs such as tarping or boarding up, and keep every receipt.</li>
<li>Report the claim promptly to the correct insurer.</li>
<li>Keep a log of every call, inspection, and letter.</li>
</ol>
<p>Our <a href="/claim-documentation-checklist/">claim documentation checklist</a> has a fuller list.</p>

<h2>Wind Damage to Commercial and Industrial Property</h2>
<p>Businesses near the refineries, the port, and along the Highway 69 and Highway 73 corridors often operate out of metal buildings with large roof spans and overhead doors. Wind can buckle overhead doors, peel roof panels at the eaves, and damage signs, canopies, and rooftop HVAC units. When the building is damaged, business income and extra expense coverage may also come into play. See our <a href="/services/commercial-property-claims/">commercial property claims</a> page.</p>

<h2>How a Port Arthur Public Adjuster Helps With Wind Claims</h2>
<p>A public adjuster represents the policyholder. On a wind claim, that can include inspecting the roof and exterior, documenting the direction and pattern of damage, pulling weather data for the date of loss, preparing a line-item estimate, and communicating with the insurer’s adjuster. A Port Arthur Public Adjuster can also look at whether code-required items, such as permits and windstorm inspection for TWIA eligibility, belong in the estimate. No one can guarantee a result. Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement.</p>
<p>We serve Port Arthur, Jefferson County, and surrounding Southeast Texas communities, including <a href="/service-areas/beaumont/">Beaumont</a>, Groves, Nederland, Port Neches, and Orange.</p>
